const ACHV = {
'金凳': { g:'obj', c:'阿散蒂', y:[1700,1902], n:['金凳','The Golden Stool'],
  t:['一张没有人坐过的凳子——阿散蒂人相信整个民族的灵魂都在里面。','A stool no one has ever sat on: the Asante hold that the soul of the whole nation is inside it.'],
  a:[['约1700年,祭司奥科姆福·阿诺基宣称金凳自天而降落在首任国王奥赛·图图膝上,自此成为王权与民族统一的象征','Around 1700 the priest Okomfo Anokye declared that the stool had come down from the sky onto the lap of the first king, Osei Tutu, and from then on it stood for kingship and for the unity of the nation'],
     ['它从不用于就座:即使国王也只是象征性地在它上方悬空一坐,平时放在自己的座椅上,不着地','It is never sat upon: even the king only hovers above it in a symbolic gesture, and the rest of the time it rests on a chair of its own, never on the ground'],
     ['1900年英国总督要求把它交出来供自己就座,阿散蒂人随即起兵,史称金凳之战——由太后雅雅·阿散蒂瓦领导','In 1900 the British governor demanded it be handed over for him to sit on; the Asante went to war over it, led by the queen mother Yaa Asantewaa']],
  s:['英国人没有理解他们要的是什么:金凳不是王座,而是民族本身的容器,交出它等于交出存在。这场因一件器物而起的战争是英阿之间的最后一次,阿散蒂战败并入殖民地,但金凳始终没有落入英国人手中——直到今天仍由阿散蒂王室保管。它是一个很清楚的例子:一件东西的意义由谁来定义,可以直接决定一场战争打不打得起来。','The British had not grasped what they were asking for: the Golden Stool is not a throne but the vessel of the nation itself, and to surrender it would have been to surrender existence. The war it caused was the last between Britain and Asante; Asante lost and was absorbed into the colony, but the stool never fell into British hands and is still kept by the Asante royal house. It is a very clear case of how much can turn on who gets to say what an object means — including whether a war happens at all.'] },
'巨型方尖碑': { g:'obj', c:'阿克苏姆', y:[300,400], n:['阿克苏姆方尖碑','The stelae of Aksum'],
  t:['整块花岗岩凿出来的高塔,表面刻成一栋多层楼房的样子,底下是王陵。','Towers cut from single blocks of granite, their faces carved to look like multi-storey buildings, with royal tombs beneath.'],
  a:[['约4世纪立,最大的一根长约三十三米、重五百吨上下,是人类试图竖立过的最大整块石头之一——它很可能在竖立时就断了,真正立起来的最高一根约二十四米','Raised around the fourth century; the largest is some thirty-three metres and around five hundred tonnes, among the largest single stones people have ever tried to stand upright — it probably broke in the attempt, and the tallest that actually stands is about twenty-four metres'],
     ['碑面刻着假门与一排排假窗,把一栋高楼的立面刻在石头上,越往上层数越多','The faces carry a false door and rows of false windows, the elevation of a tall building cut into the stone, with more storeys as it rises'],
     ['1937年意大利占领期间掠走一根运到罗马,2005年运回、2008年重新竖起','One was taken to Rome during the Italian occupation in 1937, returned in 2005 and re-erected in 2008']],
  s:['它们立在基督教成为国教前后的年代,是阿克苏姆国力的直接量度:能开采、运输并竖起这种体量的石头,意味着有稳定的组织与充足的余粮。碑下的王陵与同期自铸的金币一起,说明这是一个被同时代地中海世界视作大国的政权——3世纪的一份波斯文献把阿克苏姆与罗马、波斯、中国并列为「世界四大王国」。1980年列入世界遗产。','They were raised around the time Christianity became the state religion, and they are a direct measure of what Aksum could do: quarrying, moving and standing up stone on this scale means stable organisation and surplus food. Together with the tombs beneath them and the gold coins the kingdom struck, they show a state the Mediterranean world of the day treated as a major power — a third-century Persian text lists Aksum alongside Rome, Persia and China as one of the four great kingdoms of the world. A World Heritage site since 1980.'] },
'廷巴克图经学院与手稿库': { g:'inst', c:'马里帝国', y:[1300,1600], n:['廷巴克图的经学院与手稿','Timbuktu\'s schools and manuscripts'],
  t:['撒哈拉南缘的一座商城,同时是抄书、藏书与讲学的地方。','A trading city on the southern edge of the Sahara that was also a place for copying, keeping and teaching from books.'],
  a:[['盐、金与书一起过沙漠:商队从北非带回纸与手稿,当地抄写、注释再流通,书本身成了贵重商品','Salt, gold and books crossed the desert together: caravans brought paper and manuscripts from North Africa, local scribes copied and annotated them, and books themselves became valuable goods'],
     ['桑科雷等清真寺附设的讲学圈规模很大,内容涵盖教法、天文、数学、医学与诗歌','The teaching circles attached to the Sankore and other mosques were large, and covered law, astronomy, mathematics, medicine and poetry'],
     ['手稿多由家族世代私藏,至今仍有数十万件分散在廷巴克图与周边','Most of the manuscripts stayed in family hands down the generations, and hundreds of thousands are still scattered around Timbuktu and its region']],
  s:['它推翻了一个流传很广的印象——撒南非洲只有口传、没有文字传统。这些手稿是当地人用阿拉伯字母书写、包括用它拼写本地语言的成文记录。2012年武装组织占领廷巴克图并焚毁部分藏书时,当地图书馆员与居民把大批手稿装箱、藏进民宅、再分批用船与摩托运往巴马科,救出约三十五万件——这件事本身已成为这座城市历史的一部分。','It overturns a widely held impression — that sub-Saharan Africa had only oral tradition and no written one. These manuscripts are written records made locally in Arabic script, including local languages spelled with it. When an armed group took Timbuktu in 2012 and burned part of the holdings, librarians and residents crated up the manuscripts, hid them in private houses and moved them out to Bamako by boat and motorbike in batches, saving something like 350,000 items — and that rescue is now itself part of the city\'s history.'] },
'盐金贸易体系': { g:'inst', c:'加纳帝国', y:[700,1240], n:['盐金贸易','The salt-and-gold trade'],
  t:['北边的沙漠出盐,南边的森林出金,加纳坐在中间收过路费。','Salt came from the desert in the north, gold from the forest in the south, and Ghana sat in between and taxed what passed.'],
  a:[['盐在西非内陆极缺、在撒哈拉却成矿成板地产出,骆驼商队把盐板南运,换回黄金','Salt was desperately short in the West African interior and came out of the Sahara in slabs; camel caravans carried it south and brought gold back'],
     ['加纳王不产金也不产盐,靠对进出货物两头征税与垄断金块(碎金归民间、大块归王室)维持','The kings of Ghana produced neither, and lived on taxing goods in both directions and on a monopoly of nuggets — gold dust to the people, large pieces to the crown'],
     ['关于它的记载多出自阿拉伯地理学家之手,本地并无同时代文字史料——所以细节要谨慎对待','Almost all the descriptions come from Arab geographers, with no contemporary local written source, so the details need caution']],
  s:['这条商路把西非接进了地中海与伊斯兰世界的经济体系,伊斯兰教也顺着它南下传播——先在商人与宫廷,再逐步扩散。它同时说明一件事:控制资源不一定要拥有资源,控制通道就够了,而这正是后来马里与桑海继续做的生意。西非的黄金一度是地中海世界金币的主要来源之一,曼萨·穆萨1324年赴麦加途中散金过多、扰乱开罗金价的记载,就是这个体系体量的一个侧写。','The route plugged West Africa into the Mediterranean and Islamic economies, and Islam moved south along it — first among merchants and courts, then more widely. It also shows that controlling a resource need not mean owning it: controlling the passage is enough, which is the same business Mali and Songhai carried on. West African gold was for a time one of the main sources of coin in the Mediterranean world, and the reports of Mansa Musa disturbing the price of gold in Cairo on his way to Mecca in 1324 are one measure of the scale involved.'] },
'独立发明零': { g:'idea', c:'玛雅', y:[292,900], n:['玛雅的零','The Maya zero'],
  t:['一个贝壳形状的符号表示「没有」,让玛雅人能写出几十万年的日期。','A shell-shaped sign standing for nothing, which let the Maya write dates hundreds of thousands of years long.'],
  a:[['一点表示1、一横表示5、贝壳形表示0,三个符号就够记数——记数法以二十为进位','A dot for one, a bar for five, a shell for zero: three signs are enough, in a system that counts by twenties'],
     ['已知最早的长纪历日期刻于公元前1世纪,出自玛雅地区以西——长纪历一般认为早于玛雅;最早确凿属于玛雅的一条刻在公元292年的蒂卡尔石碑上,与印度那一支各自独立、互不知情','The earliest Long Count dates are from the first century BC and come from west of the Maya area — the count is generally thought to predate the Maya; the earliest securely Maya one is on a stela at Tikal from AD 292. It was arrived at quite independently of the Indian line, and in ignorance of it'],
     ['它主要服务于历法:长纪历要标出从神话起点算起的天数,不能容忍某一位空着','It served the calendar above all: the Long Count records the number of days since a mythical starting point, and no place in it can be left blank']],
  s:['同一个想法在彼此不接触的几个文明里各自出现过,这件事本身值得记住——它说明零不是某个天才的偶得,而是「用位值制记大数」这个需求逼出来的。区别在于后来:印度那一支经阿拉伯世界传到欧洲,成了今天全世界通用的算术底子;玛雅这一支没能传出中美洲,西班牙人到来后,连读懂它的人也几乎断绝,直到20世纪才被重新破译。','That the same idea arose more than once, in civilisations that never met, is worth holding onto — it says zero was not one genius\'s lucky thought but something forced by the need to write large numbers in place-value. The difference is what happened next: the Indian line travelled through the Arab world into Europe and became the arithmetic the whole world now uses, while the Maya line never left Mesoamerica, and after the Spanish arrived the ability to read it nearly died out altogether, to be recovered only in the twentieth century.'] },
'马丘比丘': { g:'obj', c:'印加', y:[1450,1530], n:['马丘比丘','Machu Picchu'],
  t:['两千四百米高的山脊上一座石城,西班牙人没有拆过它。','A stone settlement on a ridge 2,400 metres up, which the Spanish never pulled down.'],
  a:[['约1450年在帕查库特克时期建成,规模不大,一般认为是王室的庄园与宗教场所,不是城市或要塞','Built around 1450 under Pachacuti; it is small, and is generally taken for a royal estate and religious site rather than a city or a fortress'],
     ['石墙不用灰浆,石块磨到严丝合缝,地震时石块会跳动再落回原位——这一带地震频繁','The walls use no mortar and the stones are ground to fit exactly; in an earthquake they jump and settle back, and this is earthquake country'],
     ['1911年宾厄姆把它介绍给外界,而当地农户一直住在附近并知道它——「发现」是外部世界的说法','Bingham brought it to outside attention in 1911, though local farmers lived nearby and knew perfectly well it was there; discovery is the outside world\'s word for it']],
  s:['它之所以完整,恰恰因为不重要:西班牙人追的是黄金与都城,这处山中庄园不在他们的路线上,躲过了系统性的拆毁——库斯科的印加神庙被拆下来当教堂的地基,而这里没有。梯田、水渠与排水系统保存完好,是理解印加如何在陡坡上组织农业的最好实物。1983年列入世界遗产,今天要限流才承受得住游客量。','It survives intact precisely because it did not matter: the Spanish were after gold and capitals, this mountain estate was not on their route, and it escaped the systematic dismantling — the Inca temples of Cusco were taken apart to found churches, and here nothing was. Its terraces, channels and drainage are intact, and are the best physical evidence of how the Inca farmed a steep slope. A World Heritage site since 1983, it now needs a visitor cap to survive the numbers.'] },
'结绳记事': { g:'tech', c:'印加', y:[1400,1570], n:['奇普(结绳记事)','The quipu'],
  t:['一根主绳上垂下许多细绳,绳上打结——结的位置和形状就是数字。','Cords hanging from a main cord, with knots tied in them: where the knot sits and what shape it is are the number.'],
  a:[['数字用十进位值制记录:同一根绳上,靠上的结代表更大的位,与今天写数字的道理一样','Numbers are recorded in decimal place value: on one cord, a knot higher up stands for a higher place, on the same principle as writing digits today'],
     ['印加没有文字,全国的人口、赋税、仓储、劳役都靠奇普记账,由专职的「奇普卡马约克」保管与解读','The Inca had no writing, and the population, tax, stores and labour service of the whole state were kept on quipus by specialists trained to make and read them'],
     ['数字部分已被破译,而少数不像账目的奇普是否记录了叙事,至今没有定论','The numerical part has been read; whether a minority of quipus that do not look like accounts record narrative is still unsettled']],
  s:['它逼人重新想一遍「什么算文字」:如果一套符号能可靠地记录、传递并被他人准确复原,那么它与文字的差别在哪里?西班牙人把大量奇普当作偶像崇拜之物销毁,现存一千余件。有意思的是,它记数的方式与印度—阿拉伯数字是同一个原理——十进位值制——而两边毫无往来——位值制在互不相通的地方被想到过好几次。','It forces the question of what counts as writing to be asked again: if a system reliably records information, moves it, and lets someone else recover it exactly, where is the line? The Spanish destroyed great numbers of quipus as idolatrous objects, and somewhat over a thousand survive. What is striking is that its way of recording numbers rests on the same principle as Hindu-Arabic numerals — decimal place value — with no contact whatever between them; place value was reached independently in several places that never met.'] },
'浮田农业': { g:'tech', c:'阿兹特克', y:[1200,1521], n:['奇南帕(浮田)','Chinampa farming'],
  t:['在浅湖里围出一块块长条田,用湖泥堆高、四周种柳树固定——一年能收好几季。','Long strips of field fenced off in the shallow lake, built up with lake mud and held in place by willows planted around them — several harvests a year.'],
  a:[['先在湖底打木桩、编枝条围出长方形,再一层层填入湖泥与水草,直到高出水面几十厘米','Stakes were driven into the lake bed and withies woven into rectangles, then filled layer by layer with mud and water weed until they stood a few tens of centimetres above the water'],
     ['四周种柳树,根系把田块锁住;沟渠里的水随时可取,湖泥又是现成的肥料','Willows planted along the edges lock the plot together with their roots; the channels put water always within reach, and the lake mud is fertiliser already on site'],
     ['它不「浮」——名字容易误导,田块是坐在湖底上的,只是被水包围','It does not actually float, whatever the name suggests: the plots sit on the lake bed and are merely surrounded by water']],
  s:['特诺奇蒂特兰建在湖中的岛上,人口达到二十万上下,而周边并没有多少可耕的旱地——奇南帕是这座城能存在的物质前提。它的单位产量在前工业时代的农业里是很高的,靠的不是新作物或新工具,而是把水、土、肥三件事在空间上叠在一起。今天墨西哥城南的霍奇米尔科仍有人这样种地,2017年被联合国粮农组织列为全球重要农业文化遗产。','Tenochtitlan stood on islands in a lake and reached something like 200,000 people, with little dry farmland around it — the chinampas are the material precondition for that city existing at all. Their yield per unit area is high by pre-industrial standards, achieved with no new crop and no new tool, simply by stacking water, soil and fertiliser in the same place. People still farm this way at Xochimilco in the south of Mexico City, and the system was recognised by the FAO as a globally important agricultural heritage system in 2017.'] },
'民主制': { g:'inst', c:'古希腊', y:[-508,-322], n:['雅典民主制','Athenian democracy'],
  t:['公民自己开会决定城邦的事,官职大多靠抽签,不靠选举。','Citizens met and decided the city\'s business themselves, and most offices were filled by lot rather than by election.'],
  a:[['公元前508年前后克里斯提尼改革,把公民按居住地而非血缘重新编组,打散了旧贵族的地盘','Cleisthenes\' reforms around 508 BC regrouped citizens by where they lived instead of by kinship, breaking up the old aristocratic blocs'],
     ['公民大会人人可发言、可表决;五百人议事会与陪审团由抽签产生——抽签被认为比选举更平等,因为选举有利于有名望的人','Any citizen could speak and vote in the assembly, while the council of five hundred and the juries were chosen by lot — the lot was held to be fairer than election, which favours the well known'],
     ['「公民」不包括女性、奴隶与外邦居民,估计只占全部居民的一到两成','Citizens did not include women, slaves or resident foreigners; the franchise is thought to have covered only one or two people in ten']],
  s:['把它叫作现代民主的祖先要小心:雅典是直接参与、抽签任职、且把多数人排除在外,与今天的代议制差别极大。真正传下来的是几个更基本的东西——公共事务应当公开讨论、权力应当轮流、决定应当由被决定的人做出。它自己也知道风险:苏格拉底就是被这个制度依程序判死的,而这件事此后两千年一直是反对民主的人最常举的例子。','Calling it the ancestor of modern democracy needs care: Athens was direct, filled offices by lot, and shut most people out — very unlike representative government today. What did carry forward is more basic: that public matters should be argued in public, that power should rotate, and that decisions should be made by those they fall on. It knew its own risks too — Socrates was condemned to death by this system, in due form, and for two thousand years afterwards that was the example opponents of democracy reached for first.'] },
'奥运会': { g:'inst', c:'古希腊', y:[-776,393], n:['古代奥林匹克运动会','The ancient Olympic Games'],
  t:['每四年在奥林匹亚办一次,办了一千一百多年——比任何一个希腊城邦活得都久。','Held at Olympia every four years for more than eleven centuries — longer than any Greek city-state lasted.'],
  a:[['传统认为始于公元前776年,希腊人后来用「第几届奥林匹亚」来纪年,这是他们唯一共用的纪年方式(各城邦的月名与岁首始终没统一)','Traditionally dated from 776 BC; Greeks later counted years by Olympiads, the one reckoning they all shared, though their month names and new years never agreed'],
     ['赛会期间宣布「神圣休战」,让各城邦的选手与观众能安全往返——不是停止战争,而是保证通行','A sacred truce was proclaimed so athletes and spectators could travel safely — not an end to war, but a guarantee of passage'],
     ['393年前后狄奥多西一世禁绝异教献祭,赛会此后逐渐停办;不过没有哪一道敕令点名禁过它,另一派认为真正的原因是地方财政不再供养','Theodosius I banned pagan sacrifice around 393 and the games faded out after that — though no edict names them, and another view holds that what really ended them was cities no longer paying for them']],
  s:['它把「各自为政的城邦其实同属一个文化」这件事变成了每四年一次的实物证明——共同的神、共同的语言、共同的纪年,而政治上从未统一。1896年顾拜旦在雅典重办,现代奥运会取的正是这个意思:比赛之外还有一层「暂时把敌意放下」的约定。古代赛会的竞技者只有男性自由民,但战车赛加冕的是马主而不是御者——斯巴达的基尼斯卡因此在前396与前392年两度夺冠,是奥林匹亚第一位女性优胜者。现代这一条早已不同。','It turned the fact that quarrelling city-states nevertheless shared one culture into something demonstrable every four years — the same gods, the same language, the same way of counting years, and no political unity whatever. When Coubertin revived it at Athens in 1896 that was exactly the point being borrowed: an agreement, alongside the contest, to set hostility down for a while. Only free men competed in the ancient games, but the chariot crown went to the owner rather than the driver — which is how Cynisca of Sparta won it twice, in 396 and 392 BC, the first woman victor at Olympia. The modern games long ago dropped the restriction.'] },
'圣索菲亚大教堂': { g:'obj', c:'拜占庭', y:[537,2025], n:['圣索菲亚大教堂','Hagia Sophia'],
  t:['一个直径三十多米的穹顶悬在四十扇窗上,底下的人看不出它靠什么撑着。','A dome more than thirty metres across floats on a ring of forty windows, and from below you cannot see what holds it up.'],
  a:[['532年动工、537年落成,只用了五年;不过今天看到的穹顶不是那一个——原穹顶558年在地震中坍塌,改成更陡、带肋的形式后于562年重建','Begun in 532 and finished in 537, just five years — though the dome overhead is not that one: the original fell in an earthquake in 558 and was rebuilt steeper and ribbed by 562'],
     ['穹顶底部一圈开窗,阳光进来在下面看像是浮着;这个做法此后被反复模仿','The ring of windows at the base of the dome lets in light that makes it seem to hover, and the trick has been copied ever since'],
     ['1453年改为清真寺,1935年改为博物馆,2020年再改回清真寺——同一栋建筑的身份换过三次','A mosque from 1453, a museum from 1935, and a mosque again from 2020 — the same building has changed identity three times']],
  s:['它是东罗马工程能力的顶点,也是此后一千年里所有大穹顶的参照物:奥斯曼的建筑师锡南一生都在回应它,伊斯坦布尔那些著名的清真寺是与它的对话。它同时是一件难题的实物:一栋建筑同时属于两种宗教的历史,归属之争至今未止。1985年作为伊斯坦布尔历史区的一部分列入世界遗产。','It is the summit of what East Roman engineering could do, and the reference point for every great dome for a thousand years after: the Ottoman architect Sinan spent his career answering it, and the famous mosques of Istanbul are a conversation with it. It is also a hard problem made of stone — one building belonging to the history of two religions, and the argument over it has not ended. It has been part of a World Heritage site since 1985.'] },
'大学': { g:'inst', c:'中世纪西欧', y:[1088,1500], n:['大学','The university'],
  t:['师生自己结成的行会:自定课程、自授学位、自己管自己。','A guild formed by teachers and students: it set its own curriculum, granted its own degrees, and governed itself.'],
  a:[['博洛尼亚(传统上定为1088年)、巴黎、牛津相继出现,拉丁文 universitas 本义就是「行会、共同体」','Bologna, traditionally dated 1088, then Paris and Oxford; the Latin universitas simply meant a guild or corporation'],
     ['关键的一步是「学位」:一份由这个共同体签发、在别处也认的资格证明,老师因此可以跨城任教','The decisive invention was the degree: a qualification issued by the corporation and recognised elsewhere, which let a master teach in another city'],
     ['它有自治特权与自己的法庭,与所在城市时常冲突——1229年巴黎的师生曾集体罢课出走两年','It held privileges and courts of its own and clashed with its host cities; in 1229 the masters and students of Paris walked out for two years']],
  s:['把它说成「世界上第一所大学」并不准确:更早的高等教育机构不少,摩洛哥的卡鲁因(859年)与开罗的爱资哈尔都比它久远。欧洲这一套的特别之处在于法人结构——一个不依附于某位老师、某座寺院或某个国王而独立存在的团体,人换了它还在。正是这一点让它能连续运转近千年,并被后来的整个世界照抄。','Calling it the first university in the world is not accurate: older institutions of higher learning exist, and al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco (859) and al-Azhar in Cairo are both older. What was distinctive about the European form is its corporate structure — a body that exists independently of any particular teacher, monastery or king, and survives the replacement of all its members. That is what let it run for the better part of a thousand years, and what the rest of the world later copied.'] },
'透视法': { g:'tech', c:'文艺复兴意大利', y:[1413,1600], n:['线性透视法','Linear perspective'],
  t:['向正前方远去的线都交在一个点上——画面因此有了深度。','The lines running straight away from you all meet at a single point, and the picture acquires depth.'],
  a:[['约1413年布鲁内莱斯基在佛罗伦萨做了一次公开演示:在木板上画洗礼堂,板上钻孔,观者从背面透过孔看镜中的画,与真实景象重合','Around 1413 Brunelleschi staged a demonstration in Florence: he painted the baptistery on a panel, bored a hole through it, and had viewers look through from behind at the painting in a mirror, matching the real building'],
     ['1435年阿尔贝蒂写成《论绘画》,把这套做法写成可教的规则——从此它不再是某个人的诀窍','In 1435 Alberti wrote On Painting and turned the practice into teachable rules, so that it stopped being one man\'s trick'],
     ['它本质上是几何:定一条地平线、一个灭点,其余按比例推——所以画家在这个时期开始学数学','It is geometry underneath: fix a horizon and a vanishing point and the rest follows by proportion — which is why painters in this period began learning mathematics']],
  s:['它改变的不只是画得像不像,而是画面与观看者的关系:透视法假定有一个站在特定位置的人在看,整幅画是为那一双眼睛组织的。这个「唯一视点」的想法后来渗进制图、舞台设计、建筑图纸,直到今天的三维渲染仍在用同一套几何。它也有代价——中国与波斯绘画走的是另一条路,不锁定视点,散点构图能在一幅长卷里装下一整条河,那是另一种取舍,不是没学会。','What it changed is not only how convincing a picture looks but the relation between picture and viewer: perspective assumes someone standing in one particular place, and organises everything for that single pair of eyes. The idea of one fixed viewpoint went on into mapmaking, stage design and architectural drawing, and the same geometry is still running inside three-dimensional rendering today. It had a cost — Chinese and Persian painting did not fix the viewpoint at all, and a scattered-point handscroll can hold an entire river. That is a different trade-off, not a failure to learn.'] },
'驿站系统': { g:'inst', c:'蒙古帝国', y:[1234,1368], n:['站赤(驿站系统)','The yam post system'],
  t:['每隔一段路设一站,备好马与草料——换马不换人,消息就能跑得比军队快。','A station every stage of the road, stocked with horses and fodder: change mounts, not riders, and news outruns armies.'],
  a:[['1234年前后窝阔台把它制度化:沿主要道路每三四十到六十公里设一站,视地形而定,备马匹、食宿与向导','Ögedei systematised it around 1234: a station every thirty to sixty kilometres along the main roads, depending on the ground, with horses, food, lodging and guides'],
     ['凭「牌子」(蒙语 gerege)通行,牌上刻着可调用的资源等级,没有牌子的人不能使用','Travel required a paiza, an inscribed tablet stating what you were entitled to draw; without one you could not use the system'],
     ['马可·波罗之前,柏朗嘉宾与鲁不鲁乞已亲身乘驿站往返并写进报告;马可·波罗的记述最详细、传播也最广','Carpini and Rubruck had already ridden the relays and written them up before Marco Polo came; his account is the fullest and by far the most widely read']],
  s:['蒙古帝国大到没有任何一支军队能守住全境,它能维持统治靠的是「消息比敌人快」——驿站是这套逻辑的物理实现。它同时也是商路与情报网:元朝的疆域管理、后来俄国的驿马制度、乃至清代的驿站,都在同一条思路上。副作用同样真实:沿线百姓要负担供养,史料里对此抱怨甚多。','The Mongol empire was too large for any army to hold everywhere, and what held it together was that news moved faster than enemies — the post stations were that idea made physical. They doubled as trade route and intelligence network, and the same thinking runs on into Yuan administration, the later Russian post horses and the Qing courier stations. The cost was just as real: the households along the roads had to feed and supply it, and the sources are full of their complaints.'] },
'兀鲁伯天文台': { g:'obj', c:'帖木儿帝国', y:[1420,1449], n:['兀鲁伯天文台','The observatory of Ulugh Beg'],
  t:['一个帖木儿家的王子把半座山挖开,埋进一具巨大的量角器,用它量星星。','A Timurid prince cut into a hillside to bed an enormous protractor, and used it to measure the stars.'],
  a:[['1420年代建于撒马尔罕,核心是一段半径约四十米的巨型石制六分仪,一半埋在地下以保持稳定','Built at Samarkand in the 1420s around a stone sextant of some forty metres\' radius, half of it sunk into the ground to hold steady'],
     ['1437年编成《新古拉干历数书》,列出一千余颗恒星的位置,精度在望远镜发明前少有能及','The star tables of 1437 give positions for more than a thousand stars, at an accuracy hard to match before the telescope'],
     ['兀鲁伯本人1449年被自己的儿子杀害,天文台随后被毁,直到1908年才被重新发掘出来','Ulugh Beg was killed by his own son in 1449 and the observatory was destroyed soon after; it was dug up again only in 1908']],
  s:['他既是统治者又是亲自计算的天文学家,这个组合在任何时代都罕见。星表经由伊斯坦布尔与阿拉伯文抄本西传,17世纪在牛津刊印,长期是欧洲天文学家的参照数据之一。它也是一个提醒:这类工作依赖的不只是才智,还有一个愿意为它掏钱的政权——政权一变,四十米的象限仪也保不住。','He was both the ruler and the man doing the arithmetic, a combination rare in any century. His tables travelled west through Istanbul and in Arabic manuscript, were printed at Oxford in the seventeenth century, and long served European astronomers as a reference. It is also a reminder that work like this rests on more than talent: it needs a state willing to pay for it, and when the state changed hands, not even a forty-metre quadrant survived.'] },
'《医典》': { g:'idea', c:'萨曼王朝', y:[1025,1650], n:['《医典》','The Canon of Medicine'],
  t:['伊本·西那把当时能找到的医学知识整理成一部书,十二世纪译成拉丁文后,欧洲的医学生读了它四百多年。','Ibn Sina gathered the medicine of his day into one book, and once it was put into Latin in the twelfth century, European medical students read it for over four hundred years.'],
  a:[['约1025年成书,五卷,把希腊的盖伦、印度与波斯的医方和他自己的临床观察编成一个可检索的体系','Finished around 1025 in five books, welding Galen, Indian and Persian practice and his own clinical observation into one searchable system'],
     ['它的长处是结构:分门别类、逐症对应,让人能查而不是从头读——这是教科书区别于文集的地方','Its strength is its structure: classified and indexed by condition, so it could be consulted rather than read through — which is what makes a textbook rather than an anthology'],
     ['12世纪由克雷莫纳的杰拉德译成拉丁文,此后长期是欧洲大学医科的标准教材,直到17世纪仍在重印','Translated into Latin by Gerard of Cremona in the twelfth century, it became the standard medical text in European universities and was still being reprinted in the seventeenth']],
  s:['它的影响不在于每条结论都对——很多是错的,后来被解剖学与实验一条条推翻——而在于它示范了「把散落的知识整理成可以传授的体系」这件事本身。中世纪的知识传递靠抄本,一部结构清晰的书比一百篇散论更容易活下来,这也是它压过同代著作的原因。伊本·西那在欧洲以拉丁名 Avicenna 为人所知。','Its importance is not that every conclusion in it was right — many were wrong, and anatomy and experiment overturned them one by one — but that it demonstrated the act of organising scattered knowledge into something teachable. Knowledge travelled by copied manuscript, and one well-structured book survives more easily than a hundred loose treatises, which is why it outlasted its contemporaries. Ibn Sina is known in Europe by his Latin name, Avicenna.'] },
'楔形文字': { g:'tech', c:'苏美尔·阿卡德', y:[-3300,75], n:['楔形文字','Cuneiform'],
  t:['用一根削尖的芦苇在软泥板上按出楔形的印子——已知最早的成体系文字之一。','Wedge-shaped marks pressed into soft clay with a cut reed — among the earliest systems of writing known.'],
  a:[['约公元前3300年出现在乌鲁克,最初是记账用的符号:几只羊、几袋大麦','It appears at Uruk around 3300 BC, at first as accounting marks — so many sheep, so many sacks of barley'],
     ['泥板便宜、干了极耐久,还烧不坏——两河的城市烧毁时,反倒把档案烤成了陶,所以留下来的比纸多得多','Clay was cheap, lasted once dry, and fire did not destroy it — when a Mesopotamian city burned, its archives were baked hard, which is why far more survives than from any paper culture'],
     ['它先后被用来写苏美尔语、阿卡德语、赫梯语、埃兰语与古波斯语——不同语言共用一套符号系统','It was used in turn for Sumerian, Akkadian, Hittite, Elamite and Old Persian — different languages sharing one set of signs']],
  s:['19世纪靠贝希斯敦铭文释读成功——那块崖刻用三种语言写同一件事,与罗塞塔石碑的作用相同。释读的结果是把两河流域从《圣经》里的地名变成了有账本、有诉状、有情书、有学生作业的具体社会。已知最晚的一块楔形文字泥板写于公元75年,是一份天文记录:这套文字用了三千多年才退场。','It was cracked in the nineteenth century through the Behistun inscription, a cliff carving giving the same text in three languages — the Rosetta Stone of Mesopotamia. What decipherment produced was a society with ledgers, lawsuits, love letters and schoolboys\' exercises, where before there had been place names in the Bible. The latest dated cuneiform tablet known was written in AD 75, an astronomical record: the script had lasted more than three thousand years.'] },
'金字塔': { g:'obj', c:'古埃及', y:[-2670,-1800], n:['金字塔','The pyramids'],
  t:['给国王造的坟,大到此后三千八百年里没有更高的人造物。','Tombs for kings, and so tall that nothing built by people passed them for the next three thousand eight hundred years.'],
  a:[['约公元前2670年,伊姆霍特普为左塞王建阶梯金字塔,是从阶梯到方锥的起点','Around 2670 BC Imhotep built the step pyramid for Djoser — the beginning of the road from stairway to true pyramid'],
     ['吉萨的胡夫金字塔约建于公元前2560年,原高约一百四十七米,用石块二百三十万块上下','The Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza, built about 2560 BC, stood some 147 metres and took on the order of 2.3 million blocks'],
     ['修建者不是奴隶:吉萨旁的工匠村留下了面包坊、接骨愈合的伤者,大金字塔的减压室里还有红漆写的工作队名号,是有组织、有供养的劳役','The builders were not slaves: the workers\' village beside Giza has yielded bakeries and healed fractures, and the relieving chambers of the Great Pyramid carry gang names daubed in red — organised, fed, conscripted labour']],
  s:['它把「一个社会能把多少剩余投进一件不生产任何东西的事」推到了极限,而这件事本身就是国家能力的证明:采石、运输、测量、供养上万人几十年,任何一环断了都建不成。金字塔的时代其实很短,新王国起埃及改葬帝王谷的岩窟墓——因为金字塔太显眼,盗墓者也知道它在哪。','It pushed to the limit the question of how much surplus a society can put into something that produces nothing — and the doing of it is itself the proof of state capacity: quarrying, hauling, surveying and feeding tens of thousands for decades, with no link in the chain allowed to fail. The age of pyramids was actually short; from the New Kingdom on, Egyptian kings were cut into the rock of the Valley of the Kings instead — a pyramid is visible from far away, and grave robbers could see it too.'] },
'《汉谟拉比法典》': { g:'inst', c:'古巴比伦', y:[-1755,-1750], n:['《汉谟拉比法典》','The Code of Hammurabi'],
  t:['把二百八十二条判例刻在一块两米多高的黑石头上,立在神庙里给人看。','Two hundred and eighty-two rulings cut into a block of black stone over two metres high, set up in a temple where people could see it.'],
  a:[['约公元前1754年立,玄武岩石柱,顶部浮雕是太阳神沙玛什把权杖交给汉谟拉比——法的来源被说成是神','Set up around 1754 BC on a basalt stele whose relief shows the sun god handing Hammurabi the rod — the law is presented as coming from a god'],
     ['以「以眼还眼」著称,但刑罚按身份分等:同一件事,伤害贵族、平民与奴隶的代价完全不同','Famous for an eye for an eye, but the penalties are graded by rank: the same act cost quite different amounts depending on whether the victim was noble, commoner or slave'],
     ['1901年在苏萨出土——那里不是巴比伦,是公元前12世纪埃兰人把它当战利品掳走的地方','It was dug up at Susa in 1901 — not a Babylonian city, but where Elamite raiders carried it off as loot in the twelfth century BC']],
  s:['它不是最早的法典(更早的乌尔纳姆法典已有残篇),重要的是它被公开刻在石头上:规则脱离了执法者的口头裁断,变成任何人都能指着说「上面是这么写的」的东西。这一步与罗马的《十二铜表法》是同一回事,相隔一千三百年、彼此并无关系,却都从「把规则公示出来」开始。石柱现藏卢浮宫。','It is not the earliest law code — fragments of Ur-Nammu\'s are older — and what matters is that it was cut in stone in public: the rules came out of the enforcer\'s mouth and became something anyone could point at and say, it says so there. That step is the same one Rome took with the Twelve Tables thirteen centuries later and quite independently, and both began by putting the rules where they could be seen. The stele is in the Louvre.'] },
'22个字母': { g:'tech', c:'腓尼基', y:[-1050,-300], n:['腓尼基字母','The Phoenician alphabet'],
  t:['二十二个符号,一个音一个字母——识字不再需要背几千个字。','Twenty-two signs, one letter to a sound: literacy stopped requiring thousands of characters.'],
  a:[['约公元前11世纪定型,只有辅音、没有元音,读的时候靠语境补','It settled into shape around the eleventh century BC with consonants only and no vowels, which the reader supplied from context'],
     ['腓尼基人是商人,一套好学好写的记账符号对他们最有用——文字这次是被生意推着走的','The Phoenicians were traders, and what served them was a set of marks quick to learn and quick to write; this time writing was pushed along by business'],
     ['希腊人借去后把几个用不上的辅音改作元音,alpha、beta 就来自 aleph、bet——「字母表」这个词本身就是这么来的','The Greeks borrowed it and turned several unneeded consonants into vowels; alpha and beta come from aleph and bet, which is where the word alphabet itself comes from']],
  s:['今天世界上多数字母文字都能追到这里:希腊字母经伊特鲁里亚到拉丁字母(也就是本页英文用的这套),另一支经阿拉米字母到希伯来文与阿拉伯文。它改变的不是能写什么,而是谁能写:楔形文字与埃及圣书体都需要多年专门训练,而二十二个符号一个孩子几周就能学会——识字第一次有可能走出书吏阶层。','Most alphabetic scripts in the world today trace back to here: Greek through Etruscan into the Latin letters this page\'s English is set in, and another line through Aramaic into Hebrew and Arabic. What it changed was not what could be written but who could write: cuneiform and Egyptian hieroglyphs took years of training, while twenty-two signs can be learned by a child in weeks — and literacy could, for the first time, leave the scribal class.'] },
'代数': { g:'idea', c:'阿拉伯哈里发', y:[820,1600], n:['代数','Algebra'],
  t:['不再一题一解,而是把「未知数」当成一个可以搬来搬去的东西来处理。','Instead of solving each problem afresh, treat the unknown as a thing you can move around.'],
  a:[['约820年花剌子米在巴格达写成一部讲「还原与对消」的书,书名里的 al-jabr 就是 algebra 的来源','Around 820 al-Khwarizmi wrote a book in Baghdad on restoring and balancing; the al-jabr of its title is where the word algebra comes from'],
     ['他的办法是把方程整理成几种标准形式,再给每种形式一套通用解法——重点是「通用」','His method was to reduce equations to a handful of standard forms and give each form a general recipe — the point being that it was general'],
     ['他的名字经拉丁化成为 algorithmus,今天的 algorithm(算法)一词由此而来','His own name, Latinised as algorithmus, is where the word algorithm comes from']],
  s:['不解「这一道」而解「这一类」——这一步之后,数学处理的对象不再只是数,还包括「关于数的规则」本身。他的书12世纪译成拉丁文,与印度传来的十进制数字一起成为欧洲算学的底子。要说清楚的是:algebra 出自书名、algorithm 出自人名,这是两件事,常被混着讲。','Solving not this problem but this kind of problem — and after that step mathematics is working not only on numbers but on the rules about numbers. His book was translated into Latin in the twelfth century and, together with the decimal digits arriving from India, became the foundation of European calculation. Worth stating plainly: algebra comes from the book\'s title and algorithm from the man\'s name — two different things, often muddled together.'] },
'吴哥窟': { g:'obj', c:'高棉·吴哥', y:[1113,2025], n:['吴哥窟','Angkor Wat'],
  t:['一般认为是世界上占地最大的宗教建筑:先是毗湿奴神庙,后来改成佛寺,从没被真正废弃过。','Generally reckoned the largest religious monument in the world by area: first a Vishnu temple, later a Buddhist one, and never truly abandoned.'],
  a:[['约1113—1150年苏利耶跋摩二世在位期间建成,朝西——多数高棉神庙朝东,为什么它朝西至今有争论','Built under Suryavarman II, who reigned from about 1113 to 1150, and facing west, where most Khmer temples face east; why is still argued over'],
     ['砂岩垒成,石块之间几乎不用灰浆;外围护城河周长约五点五公里,浮雕沿着一圈187×215米的回廊铺开,总面积近千平方米','Sandstone laid almost without mortar; the moat around it runs some 5.5 km, and the reliefs run right around a gallery of 187 by 215 metres, close to a thousand square metres of carving'],
     ['高棉1431年放弃吴哥迁都,但这座寺一直有僧人住着——所谓19世纪「被发现」,是欧洲人的说法','The Khmer left Angkor for a new capital in 1431, yet monks went on living here; its nineteenth-century discovery was a European way of putting it']],
  s:['它是柬埔寨的国家象征,国旗中央画的就是它——以一座建筑物入国旗的国家极少。1992年列入世界遗产。近年的激光雷达测绘显示,与它同期的水利系统与城市范围远大于此前的认知:吴哥不只是一片神庙,而是一座靠水网组织起来的大城,而水网的失效可能正是它衰落的一环。','It is the national symbol of Cambodia and sits in the middle of the flag — very few countries put a building there. It became a World Heritage site in 1992. Recent lidar survey has shown that the waterworks and the settlement around it were far larger than anyone thought: Angkor was not a cluster of temples but a city organised around water, and the failure of that water system may be part of why it declined.'] },
'喃字': { g:'tech', c:'大越', y:[1200,1920], n:['喃字','Chữ Nôm'],
  t:['越南人用汉字的零件拼出自己的字,一个字往往一半表音、一半表意。','The Vietnamese built their own characters out of Chinese parts — typically half of one for sound, half for meaning.'],
  a:[['约13世纪成形:借汉字的偏旁组合,写出汉语里根本没有的越南语词','It took shape around the thirteenth century, combining Chinese components to write Vietnamese words that had no Chinese equivalent'],
     ['要读喃字得先认汉字,所以它始终是识字者的第二套字,没能普及到平民','Reading it required knowing Chinese characters first, so it stayed a second script for the already literate and never reached ordinary people'],
     ['阮攸用喃字写成的《金云翘传》被视为越南文学的顶峰,今天的越南人读的是拉丁字母转写本','Nguyen Du\'s Tale of Kieu, written in chữ Nôm, is held to be the summit of Vietnamese literature; Vietnamese today read it transcribed into the Latin alphabet']],
  s:['19世纪末起法国殖民当局先在南圻推行拉丁字母的「国语字」、1910年推广到北圻,喃字几十年内退出日常使用,今天能读的人极少。它留下的是一个提醒:借来的文字要装下自己的语言,总得先拆开重组。朝鲜的吏读、日本的万叶假名做的是同一件事,而三家走向了不同结局——日本演化出假名,朝鲜另造了训民正音,越南换成了字母。','From the late nineteenth century the French administration pushed a Latin-based script, first in the south and from 1910 in the north, and within decades chữ Nôm dropped out of daily use; very few can read it now. What it leaves behind is a reminder: a borrowed script has to be taken apart and rebuilt before it will hold your own language. Korea\'s idu and Japan\'s man\'yōgana were doing the same thing, and the three ended differently — Japan evolved the kana, Korea made a new alphabet outright, and Vietnam swapped in Latin letters.'] },
'石柱敕令': { g:'obj', c:'孔雀王朝', y:[-257,-232], n:['阿育王石柱敕令','The Ashokan edicts'],
  t:['阿育王把自己的诏令刻在石柱和岩壁上,让路过的人都能读到——包括「我后悔了」这一句。','Ashoka had his decrees cut into pillars and rock faces where anyone passing could read them — including the one that says he was sorry.'],
  a:[['约公元前3世纪中期刻成,分布在今印度、尼泊尔、巴基斯坦、阿富汗,用当地通行的语言与文字写','Cut around the middle of the third century BC and spread across what is now India, Nepal, Pakistan and Afghanistan, each in the local language and script'],
     ['石柱高十余米、重数十吨,柱身是一整块石头凿出来、打磨得能反光,顶上的狮子柱头是另一块石头,用金属榫接上去','The pillars stand over ten metres and weigh tens of tonnes; the shaft is one piece of stone polished until it shines, and the lion capital on top is a second stone, pinned on with a metal dowel'],
     ['第十三号岩刻里他写下:征服羯陵伽后见到死伤,深感悔恨,此后改行「法」(dhamma)——这是极罕见的、统治者自述后悔的官方文本','In the thirteenth rock edict he records that the slaughter after conquering Kalinga filled him with remorse, and that he turned thereafter to dhamma — an official text in which a ruler says he regrets what he did, which is very rare']],
  s:['这批石刻是印度最早的、能确切断代的成篇文字记录,阿育王本人也是靠它们才从传说回到信史:19世纪普林塞普释读了这种婆罗米文字,人们才知道那些自称「天爱喜见王」的诏令出自谁。萨尔纳特石柱顶端的四狮柱头是今天印度的国徽,柱头下方的法轮画在国旗正中——一个公元前3世纪的物件,直接变成了现代国家的标志。','These are the earliest securely dated body of writing from India, and Ashoka himself only came back from legend into history through them: it was Prinsep\'s decipherment of the Brahmi script in the nineteenth century that revealed who the king who called himself Beloved-of-the-Gods and Of-Gracious-Mien had been. The four-lion capital from the pillar at Sarnath is now the state emblem of India, and the wheel beneath it sits at the centre of the flag — a third-century-BC object turned straight into the insignia of a modern country.'] },
'"零"与十进制': { g:'idea', c:'笈多王朝', y:[400,1200], n:['「零」与十进制','Zero and the decimal system'],
  t:['把「什么都没有」也写成一个数字,于是十个符号就够写出所有的数。','Write down nothing as a number of its own, and ten symbols become enough to write every number there is.'],
  a:[['先是用一个点或小圈标出空位,再进一步把它当成能参与运算的数——628年婆罗摩笈多写下了零的加减乘规则(除法他也试了,但那一条是错的)','First a dot or small circle marked an empty place, then it became a number you could calculate with: in 628 Brahmagupta set down rules for adding, subtracting and multiplying with zero — he tried division too, and got that one wrong'],
     ['与它配套的是位值制:同一个符号摆在不同位置代表不同大小,所以不必为每个数量另造符号','Its other half is place value: the same symbol means different amounts depending on where it sits, so you never need a new symbol for a bigger number'],
     ['经阿拉伯世界西传,欧洲称之为「阿拉伯数字」,而阿拉伯人自己称之为「印度数字」','It reached Europe through the Arab world, which is why Europeans call the digits Arabic — while the Arabs themselves called them Indian']],
  s:['有了零和位值,竖式计算才成立,代数与此后的一切计算才变得可行:拿罗马数字做一次乘法就知道差别有多大。玛雅人也独立用过零,但那一套没有传出中美洲。欧洲接受它花了几百年——斐波那契1202年的《计算之书》大力推荐,而一些城市一度以防伪为由禁止商人使用,直到15世纪才真正普及。','With zero and place value you can do arithmetic in columns, and algebra and everything computational after it becomes possible: try one multiplication in Roman numerals and the difference is obvious. The Maya used a zero independently, but that system never left Mesoamerica. Europe took centuries to accept it — Fibonacci\'s book of 1202 argued hard for it, some cities banned merchants from using it for fear of forgery, and it only became normal in the fifteenth century.'] },
'泰姬陵': { g:'obj', c:'莫卧儿帝国', y:[1632,1653], n:['泰姬陵','The Taj Mahal'],
  t:['沙贾汗为亡妻建的陵墓,建了二十多年,白色大理石会随一天里的光线变色。','A tomb the emperor Shah Jahan built for his dead wife over two decades; its white marble changes colour with the light through the day.'],
  a:[['1632年动工、约1653年完成,为1631年难产去世的王后穆姆塔兹·玛哈尔而建','Begun in 1632 and finished around 1653, for the empress Mumtaz Mahal, who died in childbirth in 1631'],
     ['主体白色大理石,嵌着来自多地的宝石;四座宣礼塔略向外倾斜,一般认为是为了地震时不倒向主体','White marble inlaid with stones brought from many places; the four minarets lean very slightly outward, generally thought so that an earthquake would topple them away from the tomb'],
     ['「沙贾汗还想在河对岸建一座黑色的」流传极广,唯一的来源是1665年到过阿格拉的一位法国旅行家;而河对岸挖出来的是一座花园,所谓黑色大理石遗迹其实是变了色的白石','The story that Shah Jahan meant to build a black twin across the river is widely told; its only source is a French traveller who visited Agra in 1665, and what was actually dug up across the river is a garden — the supposed black marble turned out to be discoloured white']],
  s:['它把波斯的陵墓形制、印度的石作工艺与伊斯兰的几何装饰合成了一种新东西,通常被视为印度-伊斯兰建筑的顶点。1983年列入世界遗产,今天是印度最主要的旅游收入来源之一,也因此长期要对付空气污染让大理石泛黄的问题。沙贾汗晚年被儿子奥朗则布囚禁在阿格拉堡,据说从那里的窗口能望见这座陵。','It fuses a Persian tomb plan, Indian stonework and Islamic geometry into something new, and is usually taken as the high point of Indo-Islamic architecture. A World Heritage site since 1983, it is one of India\'s largest sources of tourist revenue, and for the same reason has spent decades fighting the way air pollution yellows its marble. Shah Jahan spent his last years imprisoned by his son Aurangzeb in the fort at Agra, from whose windows, it is said, the tomb can be seen.'] },
'犍陀罗佛像艺术': { g:'obj', c:'贵霜帝国', y:[50,400], n:['犍陀罗佛像艺术','Gandhara Buddhist sculpture'],
  t:['佛开始有了人的样子——而那张脸上带着希腊雕像的影子。','This is where the Buddha began to be shown as a person — and the face carries an echo of Greek sculpture.'],
  a:[['一般认为早期佛教不造佛像,而用足印、法轮、菩提树代表佛;公元1—2世纪起才出现人形的佛','Early Buddhism is generally held not to have depicted the Buddha at all, using a footprint, a wheel or the bodhi tree instead; human figures appear only from the first and second centuries AD'],
     ['犍陀罗(今巴基斯坦西北与阿富汗东部)在亚历山大东征后长期处在希腊化影响下,佛像的卷发、衣褶与面部处理都用了希腊雕塑的办法','Gandhara — today\'s northwest Pakistan and eastern Afghanistan — lay for centuries under Greek influence after Alexander, and the wavy hair, the folds of cloth and the modelling of the face are all done in Greek ways'],
     ['究竟是犍陀罗先造出人形佛像,还是南边的秣菟罗先造,学界至今没有定论——两地风格差别很大,可能各自发展','Whether Gandhara or Mathura to the south made the first human Buddha is still unsettled; the two styles differ so much that they may have developed separately']],
  s:['贵霜横跨中亚与北印度,这批造像沿丝路东传,经于阗、敦煌进入中国,再到朝鲜与日本——东亚寺庙里佛像的样子,源头能一路追到这里。它是一次看得见的跨文明合成:希腊的造型手法、印度的宗教内容、中亚的商路,三者缺一不可,而这三样凑在一起只是因为一个帝国恰好同时压着这几片地方。','The Kushans straddled Central Asia and northern India, and these images travelled east along the Silk Road through Khotan and Dunhuang into China, then to Korea and Japan — the look of a Buddha in an East Asian temple traces back to here. It is a fusion you can see with your eyes: Greek modelling, Indian religion, Central Asian trade routes, none of the three dispensable — and the three met only because one empire happened to sit on all of them at once.'] },
'甲骨文': { g:'tech', c:'商', y:[-1250,-1046], n:['甲骨文','Oracle-bone script'],
  t:['刻在龟甲与牛肩胛骨上的字,是商王问神的记录——也是汉字能一路读到今天的起点。','Characters cut into turtle shells and ox shoulder blades, recording what Shang kings asked of their gods — and the beginning of a script still read today.'],
  a:[['商王遇事先占卜:把要问的事刻在甲骨上,烧灼取兆,再把应验与否刻上去,所以留下来的是一份份问答记录','Shang kings divined before acting: the question was cut into the bone, heat was applied to crack it, and the outcome was cut in afterwards — so what survives is a run of questions and answers'],
     ['1899年王懿荣最早认出这些「龙骨」上刻的是文字(药铺买药时偶然发现的说法流传极广,但出自后人追述);此后在安阳殷墟陆续出土逾十五万片','In 1899 Wang Yirong was the first to recognise that these dragon bones carried writing — the much-repeated story that he spotted it in a pharmacy is a later retelling — and more than 150,000 pieces have since come out of the ruins at Anyang'],
     ['已发现的单字约四千多个,其中已释读的约三分之一,真正无争议的更少——大量人名地名至今读不出来','Some four thousand distinct characters have been found and roughly a third have been identified, fewer still beyond dispute; a great many names of people and places still cannot be deciphered']],
  s:['它把商朝从传说变成了可查的历史:此前《史记》所记的商王世系无从验证,而甲骨上的先王名号与之大体吻合。字形上今天的汉字与它一脉相承——「日」「月」「水」「火」这些字,三千年前的写法与现在仍能对上,这是小孩最容易亲眼看出的一条连续性。2017年甲骨文列入《世界记忆名录》。','It turned the Shang from legend into checkable history: the king list in the Records of the Grand Historian had no independent confirmation until the ancestral names on the bones broadly matched it. And the shapes carry straight through to today — sun, moon, water, fire are still recognisably the same characters after three thousand years, which is the easiest continuity for a child to see with their own eyes. The bones were added to the Memory of the World register in 2017.'] },
'大运河': { g:'tech', c:'隋', y:[605,1855], n:['大运河','The Grand Canal'],
  t:['六年里挖通五大水系,让南方的粮食能一路运到北方的都城。','Five river systems joined in six years, so that grain from the south could reach the capital in the north by water.'],
  a:[['605年起分段开凿,以洛阳为中心,南到余杭、北抵涿郡,全长约两千七百公里','Digging began in 605 in sections, centred on Luoyang and running from Hangzhou in the south to near Beijing in the north — some 2,700 km in all'],
     ['它不是从零挖起:把春秋以来各段旧渠连缀、疏浚、取直,再补上中间缺口','It was not dug from nothing: older canals going back centuries were linked, dredged, straightened and the gaps between them filled in'],
     ['代价极大——短期内征发民夫数百万,是隋朝二世而亡的原因之一','The cost was enormous: millions were conscripted to dig in a few years, one of the reasons the Sui fell in its second reign']],
  s:['南方产粮、北方驻军建都,这个矛盾靠一条水道解决了一千三百年:唐宋的漕运、元明清的漕粮都走它。元朝把河道取直改经山东、不再绕行洛阳,今天说的「京杭大运河」是那一次改线的结果。1855年黄河改道冲断山东段,海运(1826年起已试行)从此取代漕河成为主路。2014年列入世界遗产,南段至今通航。','Grain grew in the south while armies and capitals sat in the north, and one waterway held that contradiction together for thirteen centuries — the Tang, Song, Yuan, Ming and Qing all shipped their tax grain along it. The Yuan straightened the route through Shandong and stopped detouring via Luoyang, which is why the canal is now called the Beijing–Hangzhou Grand Canal. When the Yellow River shifted course in 1855 it cut the Shandong section, and sea transport — tried since 1826 — took over as the main route. It became a World Heritage site in 2014, and the southern reaches still carry boats.'] },
'交子': { g:'inst', c:'北宋', y:[1023,1160], n:['交子(纸币)','Jiaozi (paper money)'],
  t:['一般认为是世界上最早由官府发行的纸币,出现在四川。','Generally taken to be the earliest paper money issued by a government, and it appeared in Sichuan.'],
  a:[['四川用铁钱,重而不便——买一匹布要背上百斤钱,商人于是互相开具可兑换的凭票,这就是交子的由来','Sichuan used iron coin, heavy and awkward: a bolt of cloth cost tens of kilos of it, so merchants began writing each other redeemable notes — and that is where jiaozi came from'],
     ['1023年官府设「益州交子务」接管发行,定下面额、准备金与三年一换的期限','In 1023 the state set up a bureau at Yizhou to take the issue over, fixing denominations, a reserve, and a three-year term after which notes were exchanged'],
     ['后来财政吃紧、发行量失控,交子贬值;南宋改发「会子」,走了同样一条路','Later, under fiscal pressure, the issue ran out of control and the notes lost value; the Southern Song replaced them with huizi, which went the same way']],
  s:['纸币的逻辑在这里第一次被完整地试过一遍:它省下的是搬运成本,靠的是发行方的信用,而信用一旦被财政透支就会崩——这三句话此后在元朝的交钞、明朝的大明宝钞上原样重演。马可·波罗在元朝见到纸币时大为惊讶,他的记述是欧洲人最早听说这件事的渠道之一;欧洲自己发行纸币要再等几百年。','The whole logic of paper money was worked through here for the first time: it saves the cost of carrying value, it runs on the issuer\'s credit, and it collapses once that credit is spent on the deficit — three sentences that played out again, unchanged, with the Yuan and Ming issues. Marco Polo was astonished to find paper money in Yuan China, and his account is one of the first ways Europeans heard of the idea; Europe would not issue its own for centuries.'] },
'活字印刷': { g:'tech', c:'北宋', y:[1041,1450], n:['活字印刷','Movable type'],
  t:['一个字一个印,排完一版拆开还能再用——毕昇用胶泥做的。','One character, one piece of type: take the page apart when you are done and use them again. Bi Sheng made his out of clay.'],
  a:[['约1041—1048年间,布衣毕昇以胶泥刻字、火烧使硬,排在铁板上用松脂固定,印完加热取下','Between about 1041 and 1048 Bi Sheng, a commoner, cut characters in clay and fired them hard, set them on an iron plate in pine resin, and warmed the plate to release them afterwards'],
     ['这件事几乎只见于沈括《梦溪笔谈》的记载;此外他留下的只有一方1990年在湖北英山出土、被鉴定为宋代的墓碑','Almost the whole account rests on one passage in Shen Kuo\'s Dream Pool Essays; the only other thing he left is a gravestone unearthed in Hubei in 1990 and dated to the Song'],
     ['汉字数量太多,拆排一版的成本未必低于雕版,所以中国此后仍以雕版为主','With so many characters to sort, setting a page was not obviously cheaper than cutting a woodblock, and China went on printing mostly from blocks']],
  s:['「可以重排」这个想法本身是印刷史的分水岭,而它在哪种文字里最省力,决定了它在哪里铺开:朝鲜13世纪已用金属活字,现存最早的金属活字印本《直指》印于1377年,早于古腾堡约七十年;字母只有几十个的欧洲,活字的成本优势最大。古腾堡1450年前后的做法与东方有没有关联,至今没有直接证据,学界看法不一,多数倾向于各自独立想到——但两边解决的是同一个问题。','The idea that a page can be re-set is the watershed in printing history, and where it spread was decided by which scripts it saved the most work in. Korea was using metal type in the thirteenth century, and the Jikji of 1377 — the oldest surviving book printed from metal type — predates Gutenberg by about seventy years. In Europe, with a few dozen letters, the saving was greatest of all. Whether Gutenberg around 1450 owed anything to the East is unproven, and scholars are divided, most leaning toward independent invention — but both were answers to the same problem.'] },
'训民正音': { g:'tech', c:'朝鲜半岛诸王朝', y:[1443,2025], n:['训民正音(韩文)','Hunminjeongeum, the Korean alphabet'],
  t:['世宗大王为不识汉字的百姓造了一套字,照嘴的形状画。','King Sejong made an alphabet for the ordinary people who could not read Chinese characters — its letters drawn from the shape of the mouth.'],
  a:[['1443年制成、1446年颁行,书名就叫《训民正音》,意思是「教百姓正确的字音」','Completed in 1443 and promulgated in 1446 under a title meaning the correct sounds for the instruction of the people'],
     ['辅音的字形照发音时舌与唇的样子造,元音用天、地、人三个符号组合——它是少数留下了设计说明的文字','Consonant shapes copy the tongue and lips in the act of speaking, and vowels are built from three marks standing for heaven, earth and man; few scripts anywhere come with an explanation of their own design'],
     ['士大夫长期抵制,称它为「谚文」(俗字),直到20世纪才真正成为正式书写系统','The scholar class resisted it for centuries as a vulgar script, and it became the standard way of writing only in the twentieth century']],
  s:['世界上多数文字是长期演化出来的,说不清谁在哪一年造了它;而这一套有确切的年份、明确的创制者、以及一份讲清楚为什么这样设计的文献——这在文字史上极其少见。设计目标也写得很直白:让识字这件事不再是少数人的特权。今天韩国把颁行日定为公休的「韩文日」,《训民正音》解例本1997年列入《世界记忆名录》。','Most writing systems grew by drift, with no way to say who made them or when; this one has a date, a named author, and a document explaining why each letter looks as it does — which is very rare. Its stated purpose was just as plain: to stop literacy being the privilege of a few. Korea now keeps the day of its promulgation as a public holiday, and the explanatory edition entered the Memory of the World register in 1997.'] },
'科举制': { g:'inst', c:'隋', y:[587,1905], n:['科举制','The examination system'],
  t:['用考试而不是出身来选官,这套办法在中国用了一千三百年。','Choosing officials by examination rather than birth — a method used in China for thirteen centuries.'],
  a:[['587年隋文帝下诏由各州分科举人,605年隋炀帝增设进士科;唐朝定型为常设科目,宋朝加上糊名与誊录,尽量让阅卷者认不出是谁的卷子','In 587 the first Sui emperor ordered the provinces to send up candidates by category, and in 605 the second added the jinshi degree; the Tang made it a regular institution, and the Song added sealed names and recopied papers so examiners could not tell whose work they were marking'],
     ['考的主要是儒家经典与文章,不考专业技能——它选的是「会治理的人」而不是「会做事的人」','What it tested was mainly the Confucian classics and composition, not technical skill: it selected men judged able to govern, not able to do'],
     ['1905年废除。此前一千三百年间,它是普通人家进入统治阶层几乎唯一的通道','Abolished in 1905; for the thirteen centuries before that it was almost the only route by which an ordinary family could enter the governing class']],
  s:['它把「谁来当官」这件事从血统手里拿走,交给了一场可以准备的考试——这在世界上出现得很早。朝鲜、越南都仿行过;18世纪起欧洲人通过传教士的记述了解到它,英国1855年建立文官考试制度时,当时的讨论里多次提到中国的例子。它也有代价:考试内容长期不变,读书人的精力被引向经典与文章,与技术、商业无关。','It took the question of who governs out of the hands of birth and gave it to an examination anyone could prepare for — very early by world standards. Korea and Vietnam adopted versions of it, and from the eighteenth century Europeans learned of it through missionary accounts; when Britain set up its civil service examinations in 1855 the Chinese example came up repeatedly in the debate. It had a price: the syllabus barely changed, and scholarly effort was drawn toward classics and composition, away from technology and trade.'] },
'造纸术': { g:'tech', c:'东汉', y:[105,1400], n:['造纸术','Papermaking'],
  t:['把树皮、麻头、破布煮烂、捞成薄片——知识从此变得便宜。','Bark, hemp and rags boiled down and lifted out as a thin sheet — and knowledge became cheap.'],
  a:[['105年蔡伦向朝廷进献改良的造纸法。纸此前已经存在,他的贡献是把原料换成便宜易得的东西并把工艺标准化','In 105 Cai Lun presented an improved method to the court; paper already existed, and his contribution was to switch to cheap, common materials and standardise the process'],
     ['取代了竹简与丝帛:一部书从「一车竹简」变成可以拿在手里','It replaced bamboo slips and silk — a book went from a cartload of slats to something you could hold'],
     ['传说751年怛罗斯之战的被俘工匠把它带到撒马尔罕,不过那一带此前已有纸,学界现在多认为是沿丝路慢慢传开的;此后经巴格达、大马士革到北非与西班牙,12世纪进入欧洲','By tradition prisoners taken at the battle of Talas in 751 carried it to Samarkand, though paper was already in use there and most scholars now think it spread gradually along the Silk Road; from there it went to Baghdad and Damascus, then North Africa and Spain, and Europe in the twelfth century']],
  s:['它降低的是记录与传播的成本,而这个成本一降,别的事情才可能发生:雕版与活字印刷、科举需要的大量书籍、伊斯兰世界的翻译运动、欧洲的宗教改革传单。中国用纸比欧洲早了一千年,这一千年里书籍的普及程度差距很大。今天的纸仍然是同一个原理:把植物纤维打散,再让它们重新交织成片。','What it lowered was the cost of recording and moving information — and once that cost fell, other things became possible: block printing and movable type, the mountains of books the examination system required, the translation movement in the Islamic world, the pamphlets of the European Reformation. China had paper a thousand years before Europe, and over that millennium the difference in how widely books circulated was very large. Paper today works on the same principle: break plant fibre apart, then let it knit back together as a sheet.'] },
'罗马法': { g:'idea', c:'罗马帝国', y:[-450,533], n:['罗马法','Roman law'],
  t:['先把规则写下来给所有人看——这件事本身就是它的起点。','It began with writing the rules down where everyone could see them.'],
  a:[['约公元前450年《十二铜表法》刻成公示,平民第一次能知道法官依据什么判案','Around 450 BC the Twelve Tables were inscribed and put on public display, so ordinary citizens could see on what basis judgments were made'],
     ['共和后期随着对外扩张发展出「万民法」——前242年起专设一名裁判官处理罗马人与外邦人之间的诉讼,逐渐提炼出不依赖身份的通用规则','As Rome expanded in the late Republic a law of nations grew up — from 242 BC a praetor was appointed specifically for suits between Romans and foreigners, and from it came general rules that did not depend on who you were'],
     ['528年查士丁尼下令编纂,529年《法典》颁行,533年《学说汇纂》与《法学阶梯》完成,千年积累的法条、判例与法学家意见被整理成一部《民法大全》','Justinian ordered the compilation in 528; the Code was issued in 529 and the Digest and Institutes completed in 533, gathering a thousand years of statutes, cases and jurists\' opinions into a single body of civil law']],
  s:['11世纪《民法大全》在意大利被重新发现,博洛尼亚大学以研究它起家,此后成为欧洲大陆法系的共同底本;今天大陆法系国家的民法结构、许多术语与原则仍可追到那里。「法律面前人人平等」「无罪推定」这类说法的来源比它复杂得多,但罗马法是把规则从习惯与个人裁断中剥离出来、写成条文并公开的最早尝试之一。','The compilation was rediscovered in Italy in the eleventh century, the University of Bologna grew up around studying it, and it became the common foundation of the civil-law tradition on the continent; the structure of civil codes today, and many of their terms and principles, still trace back to it. Ideas like equality before the law and the presumption of innocence have more tangled origins than this, but Roman law is among the earliest attempts to lift rules out of custom and personal judgment, write them down, and make them public.'] },
'司母戊鼎': { g:'obj', c:'商', y:[-1200], ca:1, yk:'m', n:['后母戊鼎(旧称司母戊鼎)','The Houmuwu ding'],
  t:['八百三十二公斤的一口大方鼎,是现存最大的先秦青铜器——而它是用来盛祭品的。','A rectangular cauldron weighing 832 kilograms, the largest surviving pre-Qin bronze — and it was made to hold an offering.'],
  a:[['1939年出土于河南安阳,当时为避免被日军掠走而重新埋回地下,抗战胜利后才起出','Excavated at Anyang in Henan in 1939 and reburied to keep it from being seized during the war, it was lifted again only after 1945'],
     ['用数十块陶范拼合一次浇铸,需要同时熔化一吨以上的铜液——这本身就是一次大规模的组织行动','Cast in one pour from dozens of assembled clay mould sections, which meant melting more than a tonne of metal at once — an act of organisation as much as of craft'],
     ['腹内壁铸有三字铭文,是商王为祭祀母亲所作;鼎的名字就来自这三个字,读法至今仍有「司」与「后」两说','Three characters are cast inside: a Shang king\'s dedication to his mother. The vessel is named after them, and whether the first should read si or hou is still argued']],
  s:['同时期的西亚与欧洲主要把青铜用于兵器和工具,商周却把冶金的最高水平投进了祭祀用器——这个选择本身就说明这个社会认为什么最重要。用几个鼎、几个簋对应什么身份,有严格规定,「问鼎中原」「一言九鼎」这类说法都从这套等级来。器上的铭文是双重遗产:既记录了青铜工艺,也是汉字早期形态的实物证据。','In West Asia and Europe at the same date bronze went mainly into weapons and tools; Shang and Zhou China put its highest metallurgy into vessels for sacrifice — and that choice says plainly what the society thought mattered most. How many cauldrons and food vessels a person might use was fixed by rank, and several Chinese idioms about legitimate authority come straight from that graded set. The cast inscriptions are a double bequest: a record of the craft, and physical evidence of an early stage of Chinese writing.'] },
};
